<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Evelyn Baring, 1st Baron Howick of Glendale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Evelyn Baring, later 1st Baron Howick of Glendale, was a British colonial administrator and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> from 1952 to 1959. Soon after arriving he declared a state of emergency against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-mau-rebellion">Mau Mau uprising</a>, which led to mass detention of Kikuyu and the hanging of more than a thousand rebels. He earlier served as governor of Southern Rhodesia and high commissioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-baron-howick-of-glendale.jpg" alt="Evelyn Baring, 1st Baron Howick of Glendale" width="320" height="587" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ottoline Morrell (1873–1938) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evelyn_Baring,_I_baron_Howick_of_Glendale1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Evelyn Baring was born on 29 September 1903, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-earl-of-cromer">Evelyn Baring, 1st Earl of Cromer</a>, known as the Maker of Modern Egypt. He went to Winchester College and New College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he took First Class Honours in Modern History, and then served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>. In 1935 he married Lady Mary Cecil Grey, daughter of the 5th Earl Grey; they had a son and two daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Baring governed Southern Rhodesia from 1942 to 1944 and was High Commissioner for Southern Africa from 1944 to 1951. In 1949 he played a key part in preventing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a>, heir to the throne of the Bechuanaland Protectorate, from taking it, on the ground that Khama's marriage to a white woman, Ruth Williams, was opposed by the white-minority government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, which had recently introduced apartheid. Working with Percivale Liesching, Under-Secretary of State for Commonwealth Affairs, he persuaded ministers to keep Khama in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which lasted until 1956.</p><p>In Kenya Baring declared a State of Emergency on 20 October 1952 and launched Operation Jock Scott against alleged Mau Mau leaders, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>. He sought and received approval from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to use overpowering force. His administration created the dilution technique, the systematic torture of detainees to force compliance.</p><p>In June 1957 he passed to the Colonial Secretary, Alan Lennox-Boyd, a secret memorandum by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>'s Attorney General, Sir Eric Griffiths-Jones, describing the abuse of Mau Mau detainees; the paper alleges that Baring added a covering letter asserting that violent shock was the only way to suppress the rebellion. With Lennox-Boyd he also took a large part in seeing Kenya towards independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Baring left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> in 1959 and was made Baron Howick of Glendale in 1960. He retired to Howick Hall, the estate his wife had inherited, where he enjoyed birdwatching, and later took a post with the Colonial Development Corporation. He was made a Knight of the Garter in 1972. On 10 March 1973, aged 69, he died of injuries from a fall of 15 feet while climbing a cliff on his estate, and his son Charles succeeded to the barony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Evelyn Baring, 1st Baron Howick of Glendale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mau Mau uprising from 1952 led to a state of emergency under Governor <b>Evelyn Baring</b> and mass detention.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya-colony">Kenya Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marylebone, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.52,-0.15&amp;year=1948" aria-label="Marylebone on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Evelyn Baring, 1st Baron Howick of Glendale"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4992 5723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4992 5723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.52,-0.15&amp;year=1948">What was here in 1948? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
